1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,833
"Bias" gets discussed as a single sweeping topic, which is exactly why most teams

2
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:06,439
never get past discussing it.

3
00:00:06,439 --> 00:00:07,822
Nobody can check a topic.

4
00:00:07,822 --> 00:00:12,472
You can only check a specific, measured question, and this lecture gives you the one

5
00:00:12,472 --> 00:00:14,465
worth asking about your own feature.

6
00:00:14,465 --> 00:00:19,114
The question is narrower than it sounds, and more useful for being narrow: does this

7
00:00:19,114 --> 00:00:23,487
feature's quality, measured the way Section four taught you to measure it, vary

8
00:00:23,487 --> 00:00:26,587
systematically by some real dimension of who's using it?

9
00:00:26,587 --> 00:00:29,243
Not whether the model is biased in the abstract.

10
00:00:29,243 --> 00:00:33,893
Whether your feature's pass rate holds steady across the people who actually use it.

11
00:00:33,893 --> 00:00:35,886
You've already met this shape twice.

12
00:00:35,886 --> 00:00:39,151
2.5 showed you p95 hiding inside an average latency number.

13
00:00:39,151 --> 00:00:42,749
7.5 showed you a margin crossover hiding inside a blended number.

14
00:00:42,749 --> 00:00:46,292
This is the same trap, one more time, wearing a different topic.

15
00:00:46,292 --> 00:00:50,554
Treat "is it biased" the same way 6.6 taught you to treat "the model is smart

16
00:00:50,554 --> 00:00:55,148
enough": a sentence that sounds reassuring and settles nothing, because reassurance

17
00:00:55,148 --> 00:00:56,919
was never actually the question.

18
00:00:56,919 --> 00:01:01,569
The question is whether you have a number, and whether that number was measured with

19
00:01:01,569 --> 00:01:03,506
enough resolution to mean anything.

20
00:01:04,056 --> 00:01:08,584
Ninety-one percent looks like a healthy number sitting on its own, the same way

21
00:01:08,584 --> 00:01:11,737
7.5's blended margin looked healthy sitting on its own.

22
00:01:11,737 --> 00:01:16,552
Read the next two rows and the healthy number turns out to be an average of two very

23
00:01:16,552 --> 00:01:17,813
different experiences.

24
00:01:17,813 --> 00:01:22,341
Whoever phrases a request the way the eval set already expects gets a genuinely

25
00:01:22,341 --> 00:01:23,144
strong result.

26
00:01:23,144 --> 00:01:27,615
Whoever phrases it differently, less formally, or in a second language, gets a

27
00:01:27,615 --> 00:01:32,315
result seventeen points worse, and nothing in the overall ninety-one percent hints

28
00:01:32,315 --> 00:01:33,232
that gap exists.

29
00:01:33,232 --> 00:01:35,124
Nobody built this gap on purpose.

30
00:01:35,124 --> 00:01:39,881
It's what happens when a golden set gets assembled from whichever real tickets were

31
00:01:39,881 --> 00:01:44,582
easiest to find, which quietly means whichever tickets look most like the majority

32
00:01:44,582 --> 00:01:46,072
of the team's own writing.

33
00:01:46,072 --> 00:01:50,428
This is precisely why 4.2's coverage requirement mattered as much as it did.

34
00:01:50,428 --> 00:01:54,727
A golden set that's accidentally narrow doesn't just under-test edge cases.

35
00:01:54,727 --> 00:01:59,485
It can systematically under-test one entire group of real users, and produce a pass

36
00:01:59,485 --> 00:02:03,841
rate that's honestly reported and substantively misleading at the same time.

37
00:02:04,391 --> 00:02:08,939
This is also precisely the kind of number 8.4's high-risk tier expects you to have

38
00:02:08,939 --> 00:02:09,383
on hand.

39
00:02:09,383 --> 00:02:13,987
A hiring or credit-scoring feature landing in that tier isn't just asked whether it

40
00:02:13,987 --> 00:02:14,319
works.

41
00:02:14,319 --> 00:02:18,923
It's asked whether it works evenly, and a single blended pass rate is not an answer

42
00:02:18,923 --> 00:02:22,195
to that question, however good the number looks on its own.

43
00:02:22,195 --> 00:02:26,244
Notice the good column doesn't ask for a bigger golden set, it asks for a

44
00:02:26,244 --> 00:02:27,797
deliberately structured one.

45
00:02:27,797 --> 00:02:32,345
The size 4.2 already taught you to hit is enough, as long as enough of those cases

46
00:02:32,345 --> 00:02:35,229
actually represent the segment you're worried about.

47
00:02:35,229 --> 00:02:38,391
Pick the dimension before you build the slice, not after.

48
00:02:38,391 --> 00:02:43,050
Phrasing style is this lecture's example, and it's a genuinely common one for a text

49
00:02:43,050 --> 00:02:47,320
feature, but your own feature might split more meaningfully by something else

50
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:50,371
entirely: request complexity, device type, account age.

51
00:02:50,371 --> 00:02:54,919
The method is identical regardless of which dimension turns out to matter for your

52
00:02:54,919 --> 00:02:55,862
specific feature.

53
00:02:55,862 --> 00:03:00,410
Ask the people closest to the traffic which dimension they'd guess first, the same

54
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:03,793
instinct 5.8's workshop leaned on for a support corpus owner.

55
00:03:03,793 --> 00:03:08,286
A support lead who reads real tickets daily often already has a hunch about which

56
00:03:08,286 --> 00:03:10,116
phrasing gets misread more often.

57
00:03:10,116 --> 00:03:14,775
That hunch is a legitimate starting point for choosing a slice, not a substitute for

58
00:03:14,775 --> 00:03:15,995
actually measuring it.

59
00:03:16,545 --> 00:03:21,140
Say the honest caveat plainly, because "we haven't found a problem" and "there isn't

60
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:24,915
one" get treated as the same sentence far too often, and they aren't.

61
00:03:24,915 --> 00:03:29,510
A feature that's never been segmented by any real dimension hasn't passed a fairness

62
00:03:29,510 --> 00:03:29,838
check.

63
00:03:29,838 --> 00:03:31,151
It hasn't taken one yet.

64
00:03:31,151 --> 00:03:35,746
That's not a reason for alarm, it's a reason to run the check honestly, the same way

65
00:03:35,746 --> 00:03:40,287
an unmeasured cost or an unmeasured latency budget earlier in this course was a gap

66
00:03:40,287 --> 00:03:42,201
to close, not a crisis to announce.

67
00:03:42,201 --> 00:03:44,499
This cuts both ways, worth saying plainly.

68
00:03:44,499 --> 00:03:48,984
Finding a gap doesn't mean the feature is unusable, and it doesn't mean abandoning

69
00:03:48,984 --> 00:03:50,461
the segment that struggles.

70
00:03:50,461 --> 00:03:54,783
It means you now have a specific, fixable target: a prompt that handles a wider

71
00:03:54,783 --> 00:03:59,050
range of phrasing, a preprocessing step, a retrieval query rewritten before it

72
00:03:59,050 --> 00:03:59,542
searches.

73
00:03:59,542 --> 00:04:03,153
A measured gap is the first step toward closing it, not a verdict.

74
00:04:03,703 --> 00:04:08,143
That's the standard worth holding your own eval to, and most people running this

75
00:04:08,143 --> 00:04:12,749
exercise for the first time find the honest answer is no, not yet, which is exactly

76
00:04:12,749 --> 00:04:15,246
the finding this lecture is built to surface.

77
00:04:15,246 --> 00:04:17,133
That's a fine place to start from.

78
00:04:17,133 --> 00:04:21,795
A feature with an honestly unmeasured fairness question is in a completely different

79
00:04:21,795 --> 00:04:25,291
position than one with a measured, hidden gap nobody addressed.

80
00:04:25,291 --> 00:04:26,401
The first is a todo.

81
00:04:26,401 --> 00:04:29,176
The second is a register row overdue for an owner.

82
00:04:29,176 --> 00:04:33,616
Choose a dimension you can actually act on if a gap turns up, not one that would

83
00:04:33,616 --> 00:04:35,281
merely be interesting to know.

84
00:04:35,281 --> 00:04:38,611
A slice you'd genuinely fix a prompt over is worth building.

85
00:04:38,611 --> 00:04:43,273
A slice you'd only ever write a paragraph about in a report is a much lower priority

86
00:04:43,273 --> 00:04:45,659
than the ones ahead of it in your register.

87
00:04:46,209 --> 00:04:47,836
One thing before you move on.

88
00:04:47,836 --> 00:04:52,211
Name the dimension honestly, the one you'd half-expect if you actually looked.

89
00:04:52,211 --> 00:04:56,698
Most people already have a guess, and the guess is usually a reasonable place to

90
00:04:56,698 --> 00:04:57,596
start measuring.

91
00:04:57,596 --> 00:05:00,176
Then check your current golden set against it.

92
00:05:00,176 --> 00:05:04,551
If you don't have enough cases in each segment to say anything, that's today's

93
00:05:04,551 --> 00:05:07,075
finding, worth its own row in 8.1's register.

94
00:05:07,075 --> 00:05:11,786
Both steps together take an afternoon, and they turn a genuine unknown into either a

95
00:05:11,786 --> 00:05:16,161
clean result worth trusting or a specific problem worth fixing, which is a far

96
00:05:16,161 --> 00:05:19,919
better place to stand than an unmeasured guess in either direction.

97
00:05:19,919 --> 00:05:24,350
Next lecture moves to the last row on 8.1's table: what happens after something

98
00:05:24,350 --> 00:05:28,894
actually goes wrong, and the incident response plan worth writing before you need

99
00:05:28,894 --> 00:05:29,062
it.
